\paragraph{Readout concentration.}
While distributed image tokens follow the inverted-U trajectory, the readout token (\texttt{last\_preaction}) concentrates action-relevant information beyond the projector baseline.
Trained readout achieves peak RSA $\rho = 0.643$ at L12 and probe $R^2 = 0.671$ (L19), both exceeding DinoSigLIP+Projector ($R^2 = 0.630$, $\Delta = 0.041$; Table~\ref{tab:probes}, Figure~\ref{fig:probe_bars}).

\paragraph{Functional concentration requires training.}
While geometric peak height is training-invariant, functional concentration at the readout is training-dependent: readout probe $R^2$ shows a training effect of $\Delta = +0.045$ ($p < 0.0001$).\footnote{The training effect comparison uses internally consistent p0 probes (trained readout $R^2 = 0.731$, untrained $0.686$) for valid within-condition comparison; absolute values differ from the cross-representation probes in Table~\ref{tab:probes}.}
Training teaches the LLM to route information to the readout position for action decoding.

\paragraph{Convergent encoder rankings.}
RSA and probe $R^2$ rankings are consistent across pretrained encoders: DINOv2 ($\rho = 0.692$, $R^2 = 0.634$) $>$ DinoSigLIP+Projector ($\rho = 0.615$, $R^2 = 0.630$) $>$ CLIP ($\rho = 0.557$, $R^2 = 0.587$).
Both geometric alignment and functional decodability track the same ordering, simplifying encoder selection for VLA design: encoders that provide stronger geometric alignment also tend to provide better predictive capacity.

