\paragraph{Functional inverted-U.}
The non-monotonic pattern extends beyond geometric alignment to functional capacity.
Full-layer probe scans reveal that trained image\_mean $R^2$ peaks at L8 ($0.655$), exceeding the projector ($0.630$, $\Delta = +0.025$; Table~\ref{tab:probes}).
Like RSA, functional capacity first rises in early layers, then degrades---confirming that the inverted-U is not a metric artifact but a genuine structural phenomenon.
The $R^2$ peak (L8) precedes the RSA peak (L12) by four layers, suggesting that functional decodability peaks earlier than geometric alignment; we adopt ``early-to-middle layers (L8--L13)'' to capture this range.\footnote{L0 $R^2 = 0.640$ slightly exceeds Projector $R^2 = 0.630$; L0 is the LLM input (projector output after embedding), and the $0.010$ difference reflects train/test split variation rather than a genuine representational difference.}

\paragraph{RSA--$R^2$ peak offset.}
The $R^2$ peak (L8) precedes the RSA peak (L12) by four layers for image\_mean, with a local $R^2$ dip at L12 itself ($R^2$: L11${}=0.644$, L12${}=0.609$, L13${}=0.630$).
This offset indicates that maximal geometric alignment with the action space (RSA) does not coincide with maximal decodability ($R^2$), consistent with the view that RSA and $R^2$ measure complementary properties---rank-order structure versus information accessible to a nonlinear readout.

\paragraph{Metric sensitivity: RSA versus CKA.}
The divergence between RSA and linear CKA underscores the importance of multi-metric evaluation.

